<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Manila galleon</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1565">1565</a>–<a href="/years/1815">1815</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manila">Manila</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galleon">galleon</a> was the Spanish trade route across the Pacific between Manila and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acapulco">Acapulco</a>, in operation from 1565 to 1815. Galleons carried American <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> and returned with Chinese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porcelain">porcelain</a> and other goods. The trade linked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and was one of the first sustained global trade routes.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/manila-galleon.jpg" alt="Manila galleon" width="320" height="134" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: NASA/JPL/NIMA; derivative work by Uxbona · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:16th_century_Portuguese_Spanish_trade_routes.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>'s presence in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a> came from a wish to compete with Portugal for the spice trade. Magellan's expedition of 1521 sailed west across the Pacific and claimed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mariana-islands">Mariana Islands</a> and the Philippines for Spain. To trade with the islands from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>, an eastward return path was needed, and attempts in 1529 and 1543 failed.</p><p>The navigator <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andres-de-urdaneta">Andrés de Urdaneta</a> persuaded officials in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-new-spain">New Spain</a> that a route to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> was best. Sailing with the expedition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miguel-lopez-de-legazpi">Miguel López de Legazpi</a> in 1564, he reasoned that Pacific winds might move in a gyre, as the Atlantic winds did, and sailed north to the 38th parallel off <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> to catch the westerlies. His ship reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acapulco">Acapulco</a> on 8 October 1565 after 129 days, most of its crew having died for lack of provisions.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galleon">Galleons</a> left Cavite in Manila Bay at the end of June or the first week of July and reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acapulco">Acapulco</a> in March or April. Most were built in Cavite from Philippine hardwoods, and were the largest class of European ships built to that date, carrying 300 to 500 passengers. The great majority of crews were Filipino, many press-ganged; less than a third were Spanish, usually in key positions. Mortality was high from starvation, disease and scurvy. A decree of 1593 limited sailings to two ships a year, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a> merchants petitioned Philip II to protect their monopoly, and contraband became widespread.</p><p>Cargoes included Chinese silk and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porcelain">porcelain</a>, Indian ivory and precious stones and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cloves">cloves</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maluku-islands">Moluccas</a>, bought mostly with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-new-spain">New Spain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potosi">Potosí</a>. Slaves from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-africa">East Africa</a>, Portuguese India, Southeast Asian sultanates and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a> were also sold in New Spain. Goods crossed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> by mule train to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veracruz">Veracruz</a> for shipping to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>In 1813 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cortes-of-cadiz">Cortes of Cádiz</a> decreed the suppression of the route. The trade ended in 1815, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> became independent in 1821. Between 1565 and 1815, 108 ships operated as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manila">Manila</a> galleons, of which 26 were captured or sunk by enemies. The route is regarded as an early instance of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/globalisation">globalisation</a>, linking all the world's continents in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> trade, and economic shocks from Spanish-American silver arriving in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> were among the factors that led to the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ming-empire">Ming dynasty</a>. It also left a Hispanic and Mexican influence on Philippine culture.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Manila galleon” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Spanish silver dollar, or piece of eight, circulated from Europe to China, especially after the <b>Manila galleons</b> began sailing between Acapulco and Manila in 1565.</blockquote><a href="/empire/habsburg-spain">Habsburg Spain · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Manila galleons</b> sailed between Acapulco and Manila for 250 years, from 1565 to 1815, carrying Mexican silver to Asia and returning with Chinese silk and porcelain.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-spain">New Spain · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>From 1565 to 1815 the <b>Manila Galleon</b> linked Acapulco and Manila, the first sustained direct trade link between the Americas and Asia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Silver travelled by the Armada del Mar del Sur from Callao to Panama and on to Seville, and by way of Acapulco and the <b>Manila galleons</b> to China.</blockquote><a href="/empire/viceroyalty-of-peru">Viceroyalty of Peru · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Much of it crossed the Atlantic to Spain and passed onward to pay for European wars and Asian goods; much crossed the Pacific on the <b>Manila galleons</b>, which sailed between Acapulco and Manila from 1565.</blockquote><a href="/essays/cross-cultural-exchange">Cross-Cultural Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Manila galleons</b> carried Chinese silk and porcelain to Acapulco for Mexican silver from 1565 to 1815.</blockquote><a href="/state/captaincy-general-of-the-philippines">Captaincy General of the Philippines</a></li></ul></section>
